<question type="multichoice">
  <name>
    <text>NEET_UG_PHYSICS_MCQ_Q02 - Lift descending towards fourth floor deceleration signs</text>
  </name>
  <questiontext format="html">
    <text><![CDATA[<p>A lift is coming from 8th floor and is just about to reach 4th floor. Taking ground floor as origin and positive direction upwards for all quantities, which one of the following is correct?</p>]]></text>
  </questiontext>
  <generalfeedback format="html">
    <text><![CDATA[<p><strong>Step 1: Given Data and Conditions</strong><br/>A lift descends from the 8th floor toward the 4th floor and is just about to reach the 4th floor to come to rest. The ground floor serves as the origin, and the upward direction is defined as positive for all kinematic quantities.</p><p><strong>Step 2: Assumptions and Sign Conventions</strong><br/>- Upward direction is positive (\(+\)).<br/>- Downward direction is negative (\(-\)).<br/>- Downward displacement and downward velocity are negative.<br/>- Upward acceleration is positive.</p><p><strong>Step 3: Relevant Formulae and Principles</strong><br/>- Velocity \(v\) indicates the direction of motion: downward motion corresponds to \(v < 0\).<br/>- Considering the downward displacement from the initial 8th-floor position as the lift travels toward the 4th floor, the displacement vector points downward, giving \(x < 0\).<br/>- Deceleration (retardation) occurs when acceleration opposes the velocity: for \(v < 0\), slowing down requires \(a > 0\).</p><p><strong>Step 4: Step-by-Step Working</strong><br/>- As the lift moves downward from the 8th floor toward the 4th floor, its displacement measured along the direction of motion is downward, so \(x < 0\).<br/>- Because the lift is traveling downward, its velocity is negative: \(v < 0\).<br/>- As the lift approaches the 4th floor, it retards to come to a stop. For a downward-moving object with negative velocity, deceleration requires an upward force and upward acceleration, meaning \(a > 0\).</p><p><strong>Step 5: Check and Final Answer</strong><br/>Combining these directions gives \(x < 0\), \(v < 0\), and \(a > 0\).<br/>The correct condition is \(x < 0\), \(v < 0\), \(a > 0\).</p>]]></text>
  </generalfeedback>
  <defaultgrade>4.0000000</defaultgrade>
